/*!
\example interfaceframework/vehicle-dashboard
\meta tag {simulation, ifcodegen, qface-annotations, qface-enums, qface-structs, zoning, control-panel}
\brief Demonstrates the simulation control panel with a vehicle dashboard.
\ingroup qtinterfaceframework-examples
\title Qt Interface Framework Vehicle Dashboard Example
\image vehicle-dashboard.png

\section1 Introduction

This example shows how to enable the auto-generated \l{Simulation Control Panel}
using the \l{config_simulator_controlPanel}{controlPanel} annotation. The control
panel opens a separate window at runtime where developers can inspect and modify
all simulation backend properties without writing custom UI code.

The example defines a vehicle dashboard module with two interfaces:

\list
    \li \b ClimateControl - a zoned interface with per-zone
        properties (fan speed, target temperature, seat heating) and shared
        properties (air conditioning, recirculation mode, outside temperature).
    \li \b VehicleStatus - a non-zoned interface demonstrating
        booleans, enums, strings, reals, structs, lists, and models.
\endlist

Together these interfaces exercise every control type that the control panel
can render, including \l SpinControl, \l BooleanControl, \l ComboBoxControl,
\l TextControl, \l StructControl, \l ListControl, \l ModelControl, and
\l UnsupportedControl.

\section1 The IDL File

The IDL file defines a module with two interfaces, two enums, and two structs.
The key annotation that enables the control panel is at the module level:

\code
@config_simulator: { controlPanel: true }
module Example.If.VehicleDashboard 1.0;
\endcode

\section2 Zoned Interface with Shared Properties

The \c ClimateControl interface uses \c {@config: { zoned: true }} to enable
zone support (see \l{Zoned Features}). Some properties are marked as shared using
\c {@config_simulator: { zoned: false }} at the property level:

\code
@config: { zoned: true }
interface ClimateControl {
    @config_simulator: { range: [0, 5] }
    int fanSpeedLevel;

    @config_simulator: { zoned: false }
    bool airConditioning;

    // ...
}
\endcode

This demonstrates how the control panel handles both per-zone and shared
properties in the same interface. Shared properties appear in a separate
"Non-Zoned Properties" section in the control panel.

\section2 Annotations for Control Types

Different \c config_simulator annotations cause the control panel to select
different editor controls:

\table
    \header
        \li Annotation
        \li Control Type
    \row
        \li \c {@config_simulator: { range: [0, 5] }}
        \li \l SpinControl with min/max bounds
    \row
        \li \c {@config_simulator: { minimum: 16, maximum: 30 }}
        \li \l SpinControl with separate min and max
    \row
        \li \c {@config_simulator: { domain: [0, 1, 2, 3] }}
        \li \l ComboBoxControl with discrete values
    \row
        \li \c {@config_simulator: { unsupported: true }}
        \li \l UnsupportedControl placeholder
\endtable

Properties without boundary annotations use a default control based on their
type: \l BooleanControl for \c bool, \l ComboBoxControl for \c enum,
\l TextControl for \c string / \c int / \c real, \l StructControl for structs,
\l ListControl for lists, and \l ModelControl for models.

\section2 The TirePressure Struct

The \c VehicleStatus interface includes a \c tirePressure property of type
\c TirePressure, which holds per-wheel readings:

\code
struct TirePressure {
    real frontLeft;
    real frontRight;
    real rearLeft;
    real rearRight;
}
\endcode

By default, the control panel renders this as a \l StructControl. This example
replaces it with a custom arc-gauge editor described in the next section.

\section2 Simulation Data

A YAML file provides default values for the simulation backend, including
per-zone defaults for the zoned interface:

\code
Example.If.VehicleDashboard.ClimateControl#fanSpeedLevel:
    config_simulator:
        default:
            FrontLeft: 2
            FrontRight: 3
\endcode

\section1 Custom Property Editors

The control panel selects a built-in control for each property type
automatically. For cases where the default editor is not sufficient, you
can replace it with a custom component that inherits \l CustomControlPanelComponent.

A custom component has two responsibilities:

\list
    \li Read the current value from
        \l {CustomControlPanelComponent::backendProperty}{backendProperty},
        which is a JavaScript object whose fields match the IDL struct.
    \li Write changes back to the backend by calling
        \l {CustomControlPanelComponent::setValue()}{setValue()} with a new
        JavaScript object containing all struct fields.
\endlist

This example provides \c TirePressureControl as a custom editor for the
\c tirePressure property. It displays an arc gauge for each wheel with
color-coded pressure state (red for critically low or over-inflated, amber
for slightly low, green for nominal range), and a SpinBox to edit each
wheel's value directly.

\image tirepressure-control.png "Tire Pressure Control custom editor"

\section2 Wiring the Custom Editor

To use a custom panel file instead of the auto-generated one, add the
\l {config_simulator_controlPanelFile}{controlPanelFile} annotation to the
module:

\code
@config_simulator: { controlPanel: true, controlPanelFile: "qrc:///simulation/VehicleDashboardCustomPanel.qml" }
module Example.If.VehicleDashboard 1.0;
\endcode

The custom panel file extends the auto-generated base class and assigns the
custom component to the generated property for that specific struct field.
The property follows the naming convention
\c custom<InterfaceName><PropertyName>Component:

\snippet interfaceframework/vehicle-dashboard/VehicleDashboardCustomPanel.qml custom-panel

\section1 Demo Application

When you run the example, two windows appear: the vehicle dashboard and the
auto-generated control panel. Use the control panel to select an interface,
switch between zones, and edit property values. Any change made in the control
panel is immediately reflected in the dashboard, and vice versa, demonstrating
two-way property binding through the shared simulation backend.

*/
